<ArticleLayout slug="in-house-studio-vs-outsourced-production" ctaLabel="Model it against your volume" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">The comparison you have been shown is arithmetic resting on a number nobody published. Two multipliers decide the whole answer, the benefits load and the utilization rate, and the pages arguing this question source neither of them.</p>

  <h2>Is an in-house studio cheaper than outsourcing?</h2>

  <p>Unanswerable as posed, and the reason is worth more than a verdict would be. One side of this comparison publishes a denominator and the other does not.</p>

  <p>Outsourced production publishes rates, and at the managed end of the subscription category you can divide a fee by published hours and get a cost per hour. In-house has no published denominator anywhere: no statistical agency, no trade body and no operator publishes what share of a salaried creative's year reaches finished work. So the honest output of this page is a model you fill in with your own numbers, plus a clear map of which lines nobody can fill in for you.</p>

  <h2>Why no usable model exists</h2>

  <p>Start with who is writing about this, because it explains the shape of everything you will read.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Across four buyer phrasings searched in September 2026, 25 of the 34 pages returned were published by a company with a commercial interest in the answer, and none of the 34 was published by a statistical agency or an advertiser trade body. Every page that published a fully loaded annual figure did so without citing a source for the benefits multiplier it used, and not one stated a utilization rate.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: search-results audit run September 2026 across four buyer phrasings, publisher type recorded for every result, no domain named.</p>

  <p>Roughly three in four written by someone selling one of the answers is bad enough. The stronger half of that finding is the zero: not one page on this subject, across four phrasings, came from a statistical agency or an advertiser trade body, even though both publish data that bears on it directly.</p>

  <p>That is why every fully loaded total you have seen is confident and unsourced. The multiplier was guessed, and a guessed multiplier compounds across every seat.</p>

  <h2>The multiplier the category gets wrong</h2>

  <p>There is a published figure for this, and it does not say what the category thinks it says.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Private-sector employers paid an average of $46.60 an hour worked in total compensation in March 2026: $32.60 in wages and salaries and $14.01 in benefits, which is 30.1 percent of the total.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: national employer compensation cost series, March 2026 reference period, released June 2026, employer cost per hour worked across private industry.</p>

  <p>Here is where the category goes wrong. "Benefits are about 30 percent" gets repeated everywhere, and it is a misreading of that release. The 30.1 percent is benefits as a share of *total compensation*, not as a markup on salary. As a markup, the same numbers give something quite different.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Turn that into the multiplier a budget holder actually needs and salary understates the real cost by about 43 percent: $46.60 divided by $32.60 is 1.43.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: arithmetic on the national employer compensation cost series, March 2026 reference period, shown on the page.</p>

  <p>That is US private-industry data, and the distinction matters if you are staffing elsewhere: the ratio is specific to that market and that reference period. Within it, the circulating multipliers of 1.25 to 1.4 all sit below the published figure, at every point in their range. Applied to a four-person unit, using 1.3 instead of 1.43 understates the wage-and-benefit line by roughly forty thousand dollars a year before anything else is counted.</p>

  <h2>What does a fully loaded in-house model include?</h2>

  <p>Three layers. The first is published, the second is arithmetic on the first, and the third is where the category stops looking.</p>

  <p><strong>Layer one, salaries, which are published.</strong></p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Median annual wages in the national occupational wage series for May 2025 run $62,960 for graphic designers, $75,420 for film and video editors, $74,990 for camera operators and $114,850 for art directors.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: national occupational employment and wage statistics program, May 2025 reference period, full establishment survey.</p>

  <p>Note the reference period. Those wages are May 2025 and the compensation ratio above is March 2026, so they are separate instruments and neither should be described as current in the other's terms.</p>

  <p>Medians also hide more than they show here.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The published spread inside a single creative title is wider than the gap between titles. Graphic designers run from under $39,520 at the tenth percentile to over $104,910 at the ninetieth in the same May 2025 series.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: national occupational employment and wage statistics program, May 2025 reference period.</p>

  <p>A designer costs anywhere across a range wider than the distance from designer to art director. Which means picking a median for your model is already a judgment call, and a model built on medians is a sketch.</p>

  <p><strong>Layer two, the loaded figure.</strong> Take a four-seat unit at published national medians: an art director, a film and video editor and two graphic designers. Salaries total $316,190. Applying the published private-industry multiplier of 1.43 gives about $452,000 a year in wages and benefits alone.</p>

