  <p>Eight lines, every one of them real money, none of them with a number you can cite. Any page handing you a single fully loaded total has filled these in from somewhere it is not telling you.</p>

  <h2>What utilization rate does an in-house team actually hit?</h2>

  <p>Nobody knows, and this is the load-bearing gap in the whole comparison.</p>

  <p>Utilization bands do circulate, at 60 to 70 percent, 65 to 80, 70 to 80, 75 to 85. Every one found in this research was published by time-tracking software, agency-management software or an agency financial consultancy, with no sample and no fielding date. There is a worse problem than the missing method: they all measure *billable* utilization at an agency, and an in-house studio has no billable hours. Wrong instrument, wrong population.</p>

  <p>So the table below contains no benchmark. <strong>Every row is a hypothetical you supply, not a measured rate.</strong> Four seats at 2,080 paid hours is 8,320 paid hours a year. Divide $451,977 by that and you get $54.32 an hour paid. Divide again by whatever utilization share you believe applies to your own team.</p>

