  <p>Those three rows are illustrations of arithmetic, not findings. Pick the one matching your own honest estimate, and note how violently the answer moves: the gap between the top and bottom rows is twice the number itself.</p>

  <p>Now the outsourced side, and this comparison has to be handled carefully.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">At the managed end of the category, the monthly fee buys a block of creative hours, published at 120 and 180 hours a month on one card read in September 2026, with unused hours expiring. Divided out, that prices access at almost exactly $50 a creative hour.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: arithmetic on one published rate card in this category, collected September 2026, no vendor named.</p>

  <p>Set that beside the three rows above and what you have is one published number against three hypothetical ones. That asymmetry is a fact about disclosure, not a verdict about cost. The outsourced denominator is knowable because a card publishes it. The in-house denominator is unknowable because nobody publishes it, including the companies running the studios. Anyone converting that into "outsourcing is cheaper" has swapped a claim about evidence for a claim about money.</p>

  <h2>The seat you are costing may be harder to fill than to fund</h2>

  <p>A loaded model prices a seat as though the seat exists. Two published facts complicate that, and neither appears in the comparisons.</p>

  <p>The first is the spread inside a title, already above. A median hides a range wide enough that two companies hiring "a designer" in the same month can be buying quite different people at quite different prices, and the model you build in a spreadsheet assumes you land at the median rather than where the market actually clears for the person you want.</p>

  <p>The second is direction of travel in the occupation itself.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Graphic design employment is projected to decline about 2 percent between 2025 and 2035, a loss of roughly 4,200 positions.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: national employment projections program, 2025 to 2035 projection decade.</p>

  <p>Be careful what that does and does not say. It is a projection about the occupation as counted, not a forecast about demand for design, and a 2 percent decline over a decade is a mild signal rather than a collapse. What it does suggest is that the shape of in-house teams is unlikely to hold still for the term of a hiring plan, which is awkward for a fixed cost you are committing to for years.</p>

  <p>Structural churn is already visible on the buying side.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Two thirds of brands changed their agency model in the previous four years.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: global advertiser trade body content production research, March 2025, 50 global brands, benchmarked against the same study run in 2021.</p>

  <p>Two thirds inside four years is a category that has not settled. Whatever you build now, the reasonable planning assumption is that you will revisit it before the depreciation schedule on the equipment runs out, and that argues for keeping the reversible half of the structure larger than instinct suggests.</p>

  <h2>Fixed, committed, and actually variable</h2>

  <p>The category collapses this into two categories when there are three, and the middle one is where the surprises live.</p>

  <p><strong>Fixed</strong> is wages and benefits. They recur whether or not a campaign runs, and they recur at full rate in your quietest month.</p>

  <p><strong>Committed</strong> looks variable and is not. Subscription floors recur too, and at the top of the managed band they come with a term.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The top published managed floor commits the buyer to $192,000 for the year: a $15,000 monthly minimum plus a $1,000 monthly software fee, on a twelve-month term.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: arithmetic on a published rate card in this category, collected September 2026, no vendor named.</p>

  <p>A twelve-month commitment is a fixed cost wearing a monthly invoice. It belongs in the same column as headcount, not in the flexible one.</p>

  <p><strong>Variable</strong> is project-priced production, and it is the only genuinely variable option of the three. Whether that is the right answer depends on whether your demand is actually lumpy, which is a question about your calendar rather than about the models.</p>

