  <h2>When does in-house production make sense?</h2>

  <p>Often, and for reasons the cost argument usually misses.</p>

  <p>In-house wins on proximity, institutional memory and speed on small things. A team that sits in your building knows the product roadmap, has met the category manager, and can turn a change around in an afternoon without a scoping call. None of that shows up in a cost per hour, and it is why in-house adoption keeps climbing.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">82 percent of member advertisers reported running an in-house agency, up from 78 percent in 2018 and 58 percent in 2013.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: US advertiser trade body in-house agency study, fielded February and March 2023, 162 respondents drawn from the body's own advertiser members.</p>

  <p>That study was fielded in February and March 2023, on 162 respondents from one body's own membership, so read it as a direction of travel among advertisers who join trade bodies rather than as a current census. The multinational picture is separately measured.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Among major multinational advertisers, 66 percent report an in-house agency and a further 21 percent are considering one, up 16 percentage points on 2020, while 56 percent expect to move more digital production in-house.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: global advertiser trade body in-housing research, December 2023, 45 multinationals with an estimated 60 billion dollars of combined annual global ad spend.</p>

  <p>But the same body found the workload arriving faster than the structure.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">In the same study, 88 percent of companies said the workload on their in-house shop had increased and 67 percent said it had increased a lot.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: US advertiser trade body in-house agency study, fielded February and March 2023, 162 respondents drawn from the body's own advertiser members.</p>

  <p>Workload up sharply, and the budget it comes out of moving the other way.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Production averaged 19 percent of total marketing budgets, against 24 percent before the pandemic.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: global advertiser trade body content production research, March 2025, 50 global brands across more than ten sectors, senior marketing procurement respondents.</p>

  <p>That squeeze is the actual reason this question keeps being asked, and it is also why the pure in-house answer strains: a fixed team sized for the average week is under-sized for launch weeks and over-sized for the rest.</p>

  <h2>What is the hybrid platform and studio model?</h2>

  <p>The third option, and the one the two-way framing hides. Keep a small senior in-house team for direction, brand judgment and the work that benefits from proximity. Put the volume, the surge and the physical capture outside, priced per delivered asset so it disappears in quiet months.</p>

  <p>That split maps onto the fixed and variable table cleanly: the fixed cost is sized to your floor rather than your peak, and the peak is bought as it arrives. It also concentrates in-house time on origination rather than on trafficking and versioning, which is one of the eight unmeasured lines and, anecdotally, the one that consumes in-house teams.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Among in-house agency leaders, 65 percent are experimenting with AI and 71 percent are partially implementing it, while 12 percent describe it as fully integrated and 40 percent report faster content production.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: global advertiser trade body research on in-house agencies, November 2025, quantitative survey plus 30 in-depth interviews with in-house agency leaders.</p>

  <p>Note that 12 percent against 71 percent. Most in-house teams are partway through this, which is exactly the position where a hybrid is easiest to run and hardest to admit to.</p>

  <p>The <a href="/hub4you">hub4you platform</a> covers running your own team on a stood-up production system, the <a href="/solutions/consulting">consulting solutions page</a> covers the structure question itself, and the <a href="/resources/production-cost">production cost estimator</a> will price the variable half against your actual volume.</p>

  <p>Reviewed September 2026. Every figure carries its reference period, because two of them come from different instruments a year apart.</p>

  <h2>Questions buyers ask about in-house and outsourced production</h2>

  <h3>Is an in-house studio cheaper than outsourcing?</h3>

  <p>Not answerable from published data. Outsourced production publishes a denominator, roughly $50 a creative hour at the managed subscription end in September 2026. In-house has none: no agency, trade body or operator publishes what share of a salaried creative's year reaches finished work. That is an asymmetry in disclosure, not evidence that one is cheaper.</p>

  <h3>What does a fully loaded in-house model include?</h3>

  <p>Salaries, then a benefits load, then eight lines with no published benchmark: recruitment, replacement on turnover, idle capacity, time on trafficking and versioning, freelancer surge, software seats, space and equipment, and management overhead. A four-seat unit at May 2025 national medians reaches about $452,000 in wages and benefits before any of those eight.</p>

  <h3>What utilization rate does an in-house team actually hit?</h3>

  <p>Nobody has published one. The bands circulating at 60 to 85 percent come from software vendors and agency consultancies with no sample or fielding date, and they measure billable utilization at an agency, which an in-house studio does not have. Treat any utilization number, including in your own model, as an assumption you are making.</p>

  <h3>When does in-house production make sense?</h3>

  <p>When proximity and institutional memory matter more than unit cost, and when demand is steady rather than spiky. A fixed team sized for the average week is under-sized at launch and over-sized the rest of the year, which is why the squeeze on production budgets, down to 19 percent from 24 percent pre-pandemic, presses hardest on pure in-house structures.</p>

  <h3>What is the hybrid platform and studio model?</h3>

  <p>A small senior in-house team holding direction and brand judgment, with volume, surge and physical capture bought outside and priced per delivered asset. The fixed cost is sized to your floor rather than your peak, and it keeps in-house time on origination rather than on versioning and trafficking.</p>
